# Component extraction

Branching logic accumulating in one file is the most common way a React
codebase becomes unreadable. Extract early — it is cheaper than untangling.

## Extract when any of these is true

- **Two or more branches** decide what renders in the same region. One ternary
  is fine; a chain is a component.
- **A chunk holds state nothing else uses.** This is the strongest signal. If a
  section owns `view`, `saving` and `error` that no sibling reads, it wants to
  be its own component.
- **A section has its own data lifecycle** — its own fetch, its own loading and
  error states. Two unrelated fetch lifecycles in one file means two sets of
  everything and no way to fail independently.
- **The same JSX appears more than twice.** Third occurrence, extract.
- **The file passes ~150 lines**, or you scroll to find the return.
- **A `.map()` body grows past a handful of lines.** The per-item markup is a
  component; the map stays.

## Do not extract when

- It is used once, is a few lines, and needs no state.
- Extracting would need **five or more props** — that usually means the
  boundary is in the wrong place. Split differently, or pass `children`.
- The only motive is file length. Length alone is not the test; **state
  ownership** is.

## The test

> Does this chunk have state or data that nothing outside it needs?

Yes → component. No → it probably belongs where it is.

## Applied

`AnimalActionsDialog` was one file holding a menu, a weight form and daily
actions. Each form has its own inputs, its own saving and error state, and
daily actions has its own fetch. So:

```
AnimalActionsDialog     shell — owns `view`, renders the Dialog
├── ActionsMenu         the buttons
├── LogWeightForm       owns weight input, saving, error
└── DailyActionsForm    owns answers, its own fetch, saving, error
```

The shell became a switchboard. Each child owns its own state, fails
independently, and can be reused on a detail page.

## Rules for the split

- **Props down, callbacks up.** A child cannot close its own dialog — whether
  it exists is the parent's decision. It reports `onDone`; the parent acts.
- **No inheritance.** Components never extend each other. Share UI with
  `children`, not by deriving from a base.
- **Push state down as far as it will go.** The page tracks *which animal*;
  the dialog tracks *which view*; the form tracks *what is typed*.
- **Prefer conditional rendering for dialogs** — `{selectedId && <Dialog/>}`.
  The component unmounts on close so its state resets for free, rather than
  being reset by hand in several places.
- **Reach for Context only after three layers** of passing something through
  untouched, or when many unrelated components need the same value. Two levels
  of props is not prop drilling.
